in @prism

in @mirror/glass/ast/shape
in @mirror/glass/ast/token

# @mirror/glass/ast/shape/in — the `in` shape variant: an import
# boundary declaration.
#
# Per the per-variant file pattern declared in @mirror/glass/ast/
# shape: variants of `shape` may land in their own sub-file when
# they want to declare structure of their own. `in` does — it
# carries the @-ref it imports.
#
# `type in = reference`
#
# Reads: the `in` shape variant IS a `reference` (the @-path it
# imports). The variant carries no other data: the only thing an
# import boundary needs is the ref it points to. The substrate
# already knows `reference` from @mirror/glass/ast/token (`seq(
# literal("@"), name)`); every property already proven about
# `reference` transfers to `in` by alias.
#
# Legacy correspondence (from @mirror/grammar):
#
#   fixed in_form = seq(literal("in"), whitespace, reference)
#
# The legacy form sequenced the literal keyword + the reference;
# at substrate altitude, the keyword IS the variant name (the file
# path earns the meaning) and the variant data IS the reference.
# The substrate accumulates; the bootstrap stays minimal.
#
# Distinction from `out`:
#
#   - `in`  : import boundary — "this glass depends on @<ref>"
#   - `out` : export boundary — "this glass exposes @<ref>"
#
# Same shape; opposite polarity. Both alias `reference` at this
# altitude; the polarity is encoded in the variant name itself.

grammar @mirror/glass/ast/shape/in {
  # The substrate's declaration that the `in` shape variant aliases
  # the `reference` lexical primitive. No record body needed —
  # the variant data IS a `reference`. Every downstream substrate
  # that mentions `in @<ref>` resolves through this line to a
  # reference at the @mirror/glass/ast/token altitude.
  type in = reference
}

out in
out @mirror/glass/ast/shape/in
